    {
      "id": "s1",
      "title": "Turn the plain-English steps into pseudocode",
      "instructions": "Here is the plan in plain English:\n\n**1.** Start the running total, `sum`, at 0.\n\n**2.** Go through the numbers one at a time.\n\n**3.** For each number, add it to `sum`.\n\n**4.** When there are no more numbers, give back `sum`.\n\nThe same plan is drawn as a flow chart above the editor (open the Flow Chart bar). You do not draw it, you read it: the loop is the hexagon and the diamond, and `FOR EACH` does both jobs, so the diamond has no line of its own.\n\nIn `README.md`, type these as numbered pseudocode **between the first and last line**. Your four steps take **five** lines, because END FOR is a line too. Number every line, including the indented line and the END FOR line (not the last line of the editor). Indent the line inside the loop. The loop is always `FOR EACH` here, even where earlier JavaScript used an index. Call the running total `sum` or `total`. Each keyword looks like this, in a different program:\n\n- `SET lives TO 3` (start a value)\n- `FOR EACH enemy IN enemies` ... `END FOR` (the loop)\n- `SET score TO score + 10` (a step inside the loop that adds)\n- `RETURN lives` or `OUTPUT lives` (the answer, after the loop)\n\nBefore you submit, trace `total(5, 5)` and then `total(...scores)` with `scores` holding `[5, 5]`. The same steps give 10 both times: the rest parameter gathers the arguments into `numbers`, and the spread spreads them out to be gathered.",
      "hints": [
        "The loop is `FOR EACH ... END FOR`, and the steps inside it are indented.",
        "Whatever happens once the loop is over comes after END FOR, not inside it."
      ]
    }
  ],
